WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:07.832
So, that decade really starts in the late 60s to 1980, was a

00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:12.832
marvelous time for cell biology. I mean, this work came out of

00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:15.399
that time was a revolution for understanding how synaptic

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:21.332
transmission works. There was just an era of great cell

00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:24.499
biology. So why do you think that happened?

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.066
And why did it end?

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:29.099
Well I don't think it did end.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.166
Are you kidding me? It has just started up again.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:35.932
The focus, you know, is molecular biology sort of swept away.

00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:38.199
Oh yeah, I mean there was a period of time in the summer, of course,

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:45.032
you get these guys at Caltech. In fact, we had a student and

00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:49.932
she got up and she said, well I want everybody to know that

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:54.699
electron microscopy is a dying field. And so we wanted

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.699
-- we made a plot of what a dying field looked like over our door.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.666
A dying electromagnetic field. Remember that?

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:02.299
No!

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.999
It just showed this thing where all of a sudden the curve went

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.332
down. And it said "Dying Field." She was in Keith Porter's lab, too.

00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:10.766
Really?

00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:15.132
The founder of modern electron microscopy had become disillusioned.

00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:17.999
And so there was a period of time when people thought that

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.532
we were going to understand everything by understanding genes.

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:25.132
And we've understood a lot from genes, but you don't understand everything

00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:33.399
from genes. Now, finally, the technical side of electron microscopes

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.832
is having a drastic -- and new kinds of electron microscopes

00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:43.066
are appearing. And the challenge is on people like John and me,

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:47.966
people who actually prepare stuff for electron microscopes

00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:52.599
to take advantage of these new kinds of optical things that these

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.666
microscopes can use. And that's going to happen big time.

00:01:56.667 --> 00:02:01.699
We're going to have a whole new understanding of cells,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.532
not at the level of membranes running around, but of the individual

00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:08.999
protein molecules running around each other.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.032
Back up to what Tom was talking about, the death of electron

00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:17.932
microscopy. That period when its imminent death seemed

00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:21.632
unavoidable. There are a couple of other factors that I'd like

00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:25.132
to bring up. One is that that was a period when light

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:28.499
microscopy took off, when it first started. And light

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.766
microscopy was providing two incredibly fundamental things that EM

00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:36.566
did not provide. Number one, the dynamics of cells.

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:41.099
The actual movements of all of these things. Mind you,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:47.232
light microscopy is 1/1000 the resolution, that far away.

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:52.132
But it was a whole process displayed for you in a living cell.

00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:59.366
Second, the light microscopy was allowing you to begin to identify

00:02:59.367 --> 00:03:03.966
which protein was doing it, because new technology of fluorescence,

00:03:03.967 --> 00:03:07.299
fluorescent proteins and so on, were coming in to be examined by

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:12.432
light microscopy. So, it was not that electron microscopy died, it was

00:03:12.433 --> 00:03:16.366
eclipsed by light microscopy and the huge potential of it.

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:17.966
You went through a light microscopy phase.

00:03:17.967 --> 00:03:21.099
I fell in love with it myself, and lost myself in it.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:29.532
But, the critical thing we've always been up against, and we're still

00:03:29.533 --> 00:03:33.199
up against, no matter how fancy the electron microscopes get,

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.499
or how perfect our means of viewing is, we'll never get around

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.699
the fact that the electron microscope gives you a static

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.999
image. A still, a snapshot of something. One instant in time.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.432
For each picture that you take, each preparation that you make,

00:03:49.433 --> 00:03:54.366
is one instant in time. And you have to back extrapolate

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:58.732
to what was happening in life. You have to say, well I timed it

00:03:58.733 --> 00:04:01.366
perfectly, I had 5 milliseconds, I had 10 milliseconds,

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:05.166
20 milliseconds, so I can say it did this, that, and that.

00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:08.166
But that's an interpretation in and of itself.

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:11.899
Open to questions if you got competitors in your field.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:17.432
It's always that extrapolation event that's an interpretation.

00:04:17.433 --> 00:04:22.899
And that made people shy off and led to the demise,

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.099
of which we're now trying to recover. Always will be trying to

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:27.832
recover.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:31.832
I mean, if you were a young student in the course and you came in

00:04:31.833 --> 00:04:37.399
and you had a choice of spending a week doing meticulous

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.666
preparation to get something ready for the electron microscope

00:04:39.667 --> 00:04:44.499
to get one picture of it, or you could take some genes and create some

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:48.166
beautiful colors that would run up and down inside your cell,

00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:50.932
you could sit there all afternoon looking at them, which would you do?

00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:55.766
Got me. I'd sit here and look at this. That's one picture.

00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:59.266
So what is the future of electron microscopy then? Why

00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:03.232
-- what recommends it to students?

00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:08.499
Well, you asked two questions there. The future and

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.366
what recommends it to students. It might be that there's little to

00:05:12.367 --> 00:05:15.166
recommend to the students. That's what we're really facing.

00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:18.532
How many people are going into this field? It's heartbreaking to see

00:05:18.533 --> 00:05:21.966
how few people are being trained, even in these labs that

00:05:21.967 --> 00:05:27.232
are totally cutting edge. And the whole culture of electron microscopy

00:05:27.233 --> 00:05:31.566
is in dire danger.

00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:35.866
So still, you haven't answered why they should be trained,

00:05:35.867 --> 00:05:37.999
what are they going to see that you can't see in a light

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.166
electron microscope?

00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:46.832
Everything. I mean you're very limited. I mean the idea people

00:05:46.833 --> 00:05:50.966
got all excited about high resolution microscopy and super

00:05:50.967 --> 00:05:57.266
resolution. Super resolution, that's about a 100% improvement.

00:05:57.267 --> 00:06:01.666
You go from seeing something that's 200 nm to something

00:06:01.667 --> 00:06:06.199
that's 50nm. But we see stuff all the time that's 1nm!

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:12.999
2nm. I mean, the gap between resolution in electron

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.066
microscope and a light microscope is huge. And I don't

00:06:17.067 --> 00:06:21.332
-- my goal in my current work is that I want to understand

00:06:21.333 --> 00:06:25.499
how individual proteins fit together to make protein motors.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.699
Now you can examine that with light microscopy, but

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:33.466
with some fluorescent dyes and watch what happens,

00:06:33.467 --> 00:06:36.766
make some inferences, but you don't actually know until you

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:42.499
get the images and reconstruct the proteins.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:47.299
This is -- I've just listened to Tom give his lectures

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:51.132
to this year's students at the Marine Biological Laboratory

00:06:51.133 --> 00:06:57.332
and was impressed again by what an appearance he has.

00:06:57.333 --> 00:07:03.132
He, as he's saying, and it's apparent in every word he says

00:07:03.133 --> 00:07:07.832
to students, he believes, and I totally agree with him, that

00:07:07.833 --> 00:07:12.266
it's at the EM resolution, a level of electron microscopy,

00:07:12.267 --> 00:07:17.632
that we're going to understand cell function, organelle function,

00:07:17.633 --> 00:07:20.499
and everything about the cell. Because it's at that level

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:25.166
that you can actually see molecules. And the whole name of the game

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:27.632
is seeing molecules interacting with each other.

00:07:27.633 --> 00:07:33.066
That's -- to understand this machine called life, we have

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:36.299
to see the individual molecules interacting with each other.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.766
Light microscopy gives you these wonderful clues that

00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:43.432
they're dancing together, or going over here, but to see

00:07:43.433 --> 00:07:49.732
how that machine works, you have to have the resolution of

00:07:49.733 --> 00:07:57.032
this instrument. And it's a simple fact. It's amazing that we have

00:07:57.033 --> 00:08:00.932
the instrument. It's great that we've had it for 50 years,

00:08:00.933 --> 00:08:07.032
60 years almost, but it's got a niche in cell biology

00:08:07.033 --> 00:08:10.266
and in medical science that is irreplaceable.

00:08:10.267 --> 00:08:30.067
Never will be the same.

